# G084 — CAPTCHA Maker

*v2. Closes Web category. See `captcha.md` for prose: challenge as
ephemeral single-use time-bounded token, outcomes structurally
distinct, attempt counter as rate limit, challenge kinds polymorphic
through one generator, lenient answer matching, cleanup as caller-
scheduled garbage collection.*

---

## Choreographic Case: Gated Form Submission

[gated-form-submit @form-session
    IN ORDER TO
        issue a math CAPTCHA at session start
        require a verified challenge before accepting submission
        and refuse correctly across wrong / expired / exhausted cases

    @svc <- [captcha[new-service
                       {ttl-ms: 120000, max-attempts: 3}]]

    sync @form-events {
        start ->
            @ch <- [captcha[issue {svc: @svc, kind: "Math"}]]
            [ui[show-challenge
                 {id: @ch:id, prompt: @ch:prompt}]]

        submit @challenge-id @answer @payload ->
            @result <- [captcha[verify
                                  {svc: @svc,
                                   id: @challenge-id,
                                   answer: @answer,
                                   now-ms: [clock[now]]}]]
            @result:kind
                where "Success" ->
                    [form[process {payload: @payload}]]
                where "WrongAnswer" ->
                    [ui[show-error
                         {msg: "incorrect, " +
                                @result:remaining + " attempts left"}]]
                where "Expired" ->
                    [ui[show-error {msg: "session expired; request new"}]]
                where "AlreadySolved" ->
                    [form[process {payload: @payload}]]  ;; duplicate click
                where "TooManyAttempts" ->
                    [ui[show-error
                         {msg: "too many tries; request new challenge"}]]
                where "Unknown" ->
                    [ui[show-error {msg: "invalid token"}]]
    }

    every 1 minute
        [captcha[cleanup {svc: @svc, now-ms: [clock[now]]}]]
]

---

## Verb Surface

[captcha
    new-service         {ttl-ms, max-attempts}
    issue               {svc, kind}                     ;; -> Challenge
                                                        ;; kinds: Math, WordRecall,
                                                        ;; ReverseString, SequenceCount
    verify              {svc, id, answer, now-ms}       ;; -> VerifyResult
    cleanup             {svc, now-ms}                   ;; remove solved + expired
    active-count        {svc, now-ms}
]

---

## Distinctive Innate idiom: dual-bound refusal `where` either bound trips

[captcha[verify {svc: @s, id: @id, answer: @a, now-ms: @now}]
    IN ORDER TO
        branch into one of six structurally-distinct outcomes
        so the UI can guide the user appropriately per failure mode

    @challenge <- @s:challenges at @id
        where absent                             -> #Unknown
    @challenge
        where solved is true                     -> #AlreadySolved
        where (@now - @challenge:issued-ms)
              greater than @s:ttl-ms              -> #Expired
        where @challenge:attempts-used
              at least @s:max-attempts            -> #TooManyAttempts
        where answers-match(@challenge:expected, @a)
            -> mark solved, return #Success
        otherwise
            -> increment attempts-used
            -> #WrongAnswer {remaining: max - used}
]

Collapsing into accepted/rejected loses the information the UI needs.
Each outcome triggers a different user action: retry, request-new,
contact-support, or proceed.

## Distinctive Innate idiom: challenge kinds dispatched at issue time

[captcha[issue {svc: @s, kind: @k}]
    @k
        where "Math"
            -> two random 1..20 operands, operator in {+, -, ×}
        where "WordRecall"
            -> "Name a colour"; expected in {"red", "blue", ...}
        where "ReverseString"
            -> "Reverse HELLO"; expected "OLLEH"
        where "SequenceCount"
            -> "Count 2's in 2,1,2,3,2,4"; expected "3"
    store expected answer with issued-ms, attempts-used: 0, solved: false
]

Offering multiple kinds lets the frontend pick per accessibility —
math excludes non-native speakers, recall excludes some impairments,
reverse-string excludes some dyslexic users.

## Distinctive Innate idiom: lenient matching `where` trivial variation is OK

[answers-match {expected: @e, user: @u}]
    trim whitespace from both
    lowercase both
    where result equal                          -> true
    otherwise                                    -> false
]

Forgiveness scoped to the specific noise the domain produces
(whitespace + case). Punctuation and numeric locale are NOT
normalised — "9.5" and "9,5" are legitimately different.

---

## Demo

[demo
    @svc <- [captcha[new-service
                       {ttl-ms: 60000, max-attempts: 3}]]

    @ch <- [captcha[issue {svc: @svc, kind: "Math"}]]
    ;; @ch:prompt -> e.g. "What is 7 + 3?"

    -> [captcha[verify
                  {svc: @svc, id: @ch:id,
                   answer: "wrong", now-ms: 1000}]]
    ;; -> #WrongAnswer {remaining: 2}

    -> [captcha[verify
                  {svc: @svc, id: @ch:id,
                   answer: " 10 ", now-ms: 2000}]]
    ;; -> #Success  (lenient: whitespace trimmed)

    -> [captcha[verify
                  {svc: @svc, id: @ch:id,
                   answer: "10", now-ms: 3000}]]
    ;; -> #AlreadySolved
]
